  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe hottest day of the week will come Wednesday when the high \npressure bullseye moves directly over Middle TN. Highs will warm \ninto the mid 90s with increasing atmospheric moisture ahead of the \nnext front. This will result in heat index values reaching just over \n100 degrees for a large chunk of points Nashville and west. There \nwill be a dip in the zonal flow Thursday that will eventually push a \nfront through the area. This front will bring our next decent chance \nfor rain and storms on both Thursday and Friday with chances ranging \nbetween 50-70%. Soundings aren't favorable for widespread severe \nweather, but bulk shear will increase near 30 kts. Mid-level lapse \nrates will be poor, but there will be plenty of instability with \nsome forcing, so a low severe weather threat is not totally out of \nthe question, but the threat overall appears very low at this \ntime. For the weekend, a series of weak shortwaves will ripple \nthrough the continued zonal flow, keeping low to medium rain \nchances between 20-30% in the forecast through Sunday. \nTemperatures will remain warm, in the upper 80s to low 90s.",
